        "summary": "{\"pack_schema\":\"universe.design-seed-pack.v0\",\"pack_id\":\"grok-pretraining-design-seed-pack-v0\",\"canonical_status\":\"CANDIDATE_RESEARCH_ONLY\",\"provenance\":\"MODEL_RECALL\",\"seed_count\":10,\"source_access\":{\"web_research\":\"NOT_PERFORMED\",\"local_files\":\"NOT_PERFORMED\",\"tools_or_network\":\"NOT_PERFORMED\",\"sibling_packs\":\"NOT_AVAILABLE\"},\"seeds\":[{\"observed_example_or_era\":\"Xerox Star 8010 Information System, 1981, with Alto/Bravo lineage from Xerox PARC in the 1970s\",\"observation\":\"Office work was presented as a persistent spatial desktop of named documents, folders, and devices rather than as a command language over files.\",\"problem_addressed\":\"Non-specialists could not retain command syntax and needed a visible world of typed objects with stable locations and recognizable affordances.\",\"enabling_constraints_or_technology\":\"Bitmap displays, mouse pointing, bitblt, an object-oriented internal document model, and expensive dedicated workstations that made WYSIWYG office hardware thinkable for professionals.\",\"interaction_form\":\"Point-and-select direct manipulation of icons; property sheets; WYSIWYG documents; the desktop as spatial memory.\",\"contemporary_reaction_or_critique\":\"Admired as a coherent vision but criticized as costly, closed, and commercially weak next to cheaper command-line PCs; later GUIs were accused of copying the surface while dropping document-centric consistency.\",\"suspected_source_or_example\":\"Xerox Star 8010 desktop, icons, and property sheets; Alto icons and the Bravo editor as precursors.\",\"source_references\":[\"Johnson, J. et al. The Xerox Star: A Retrospective. IEEE Computer 22(9), 1989 (citation from model recall)\",\"Smith, D.C. et al. Designing the Star User Interface. BYTE, April 1982 (citation from model recall)\"],\"universe_relevance_clue\":\"A task world whose frames, packs, and results stay spatially stable and object-typed may be more scannable than a purely conversational transcript.\",\"inference_or_causal_reading\":\"Visibility plus stable identity reduced memory load; the metaphor worked because office objects already had cultural conventions, not because icons are intrinsically better than text.\",\"uncertainty\":\"Fine-grained Star interaction sequences and contemporaneous sales figures are recalled approximately; no primary documents were consulted this turn.\",\"provenance\":\"MODEL_RECALL\"},{\"observed_example_or_era\":\"Douglas Engelbart NLS and the Mother of All Demos, 1968\",\"observation\":\"Knowledge work was treated as structured, linkable, collaboratively viewable outline material operated with mouse, keyset, and shared screens rather than as isolated documents.\",\"problem_addressed\":\"Individual intellect and group coordination were limited by paper, sequential terminals, and the lack of an externalized working structure.\",\"enabling_constraints_or_technology\":\"Time-sharing, CRT displays, a pointing device, chorded input, and a research environment that could absorb high training cost.\",\"interaction_form\":\"Hierarchical views, jump links, mixed text-and-command interaction, and remote shared viewing of the same structured workspace.\",\"contemporary_reaction_or_critique\":\"Recognized as prophetic but criticized as too complex, too dependent on trained operators, and not productized; many later systems borrowed fragments without the full augmentation ethos.\",\"suspected_source_or_example\":\"Engelbart NLS/Augment; the 9 December 1968 AFIPS/Fall Joint Computer Conference demonstration.\",\"source_references\":[\"Engelbart, D.C. and English, W.K. A Research Center for Augmenting Human Intellect. AFIPS FJCC, 1968 (citation from model recall)\",\"Engelbart, D.C. Augmenting Human Intellect: A Conceptual Framework. SRI, 1962 (citation from model recall)\"],\"universe_relevance_clue\":\"Nested task structure, shared frames, and links among results resemble intellect-augmentation outlines more than they resemble a single chat bubble.\",\"inference_or_causal_reading\":\"Tools that externalize structure scale collaborative knowledge work, but ",
